What the AGPL actually does, mechanically
-----------------------------------------

The AGPL is the GNU General Public License version 3 with one additional
requirement bolted on, and it is worth stating plainly what that requirement
is, because it is the entire reason to pick this license over any other.

Under an ordinary copyleft license like the GPL, the obligation to hand over
source code is triggered by *distribution* -- by giving somebody a copy of the
program. This means a company can take GPL code, modify it, run the modified
version on their own servers, sell access to it over a network, and never
distribute a single copy to anyone. No copies changed hands, so no obligation
was ever triggered. The users get the service and never get the source.

Section 13 of the AGPL closes that. If somebody modifies this code and then
lets people interact with it remotely over a network, that interaction itself
triggers the obligation: they must offer those users the corresponding source
of their modified version, through the network, at no charge. Running it as a
service is treated the same as handing out copies.

The other terms are the standard GPLv3 ones. Anybody may run, read, modify, and
redistribute this. If they redistribute it, or run a modified version as a
network service, they must do so under this same license, with source
available. They cannot add restrictions on top. There is no warranty. Their
patent rights in what they contribute pass along with the code.

The practical effect is that this work stays open in every direction it can
travel. Someone can build on it, including commercially; what they cannot do is
close it, or wrap it in a service and keep the improvements to themselves.

Work in this repository that I did not write
--------------------------------------------

I cannot license code I did not write, and I am not going to pretend otherwise.

Anything in these directories that came from somewhere else is covered by
whatever license its original author gave it, not by mine. Where a directory
carries its own license file, that file governs that directory and this one
does not reach inside it. This applies to vendored dependencies, upstream
checkouts, downloaded art assets, toolchains, and anything else that arrived
here already licensed -- including cases where the original terms are
proprietary and could not be combined with the AGPL even if I wanted to.

It also applies to a few of my own projects that carry a different license of
their own choosing. Those are mine either way, so there is no conflict; the
subdirectory license simply wins where one exists.

The rule is: look for the nearest license file, walking up from whatever you
are holding, and read the file's own header while you are at it -- plenty of
single-file libraries carry their entire grant inline as a comment and never
ship a separate license file at all. If you find terms before you reach this
directory, those are the ones that apply. If you get all the way here without
finding anything, the work is mine and it is AGPL-3.0-or-later.

One correction to that rule, and it matters more than the rule does: a missing
license file does not make somebody else's work mine. If a vendored library or
a downloaded asset landed here without its license attached, that is a bug in
how I stored it, not a transfer of ownership. It stays under whatever terms its
author actually granted, and the fix is to go put the license file where it
belongs. Nothing in this document is a claim over work I did not do.

